		<h1 class="largetext">MASSLINE</h1>
		<h2 class="normaltext">MASS LINE FORUMS => The Business => Topic started by: martyfever on August 09, 2007, 09:56:42 pm</h2>

		<table width="90%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0">
			<tr>
				<td>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>martyfever</b> on <b>August 09, 2007, 09:56:42 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">My name is Martin Feveyear, I&#039;m a producer &amp; mixer who mixed the last Common Market CD and &quot;Bayani&quot; for the Blue Scholars. I have been producing, engineering &amp; mixing records of all genres for the last 15 years. Although I tend to stay out of the business side of things, this forum seems to be a good place to discuss the technical and musical aspects of record making. After mixing those two CDs I have been receiving a lot of enquiries from hip-hop artists interested in mixing and mastering. The vibe I get from some of these artists is that they are unaware or unsure of the process. If anyone is interested I&#039;d be happy to answer any questions concerning recording, mixing, mastering and the possible benefits and costs of working with someone like myself.<br />Martin</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Jawn</b> on <b>August 09, 2007, 09:58:43 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">How about lessons?</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>M</b> on <b>August 09, 2007, 10:07:33 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Hey Martin -- welcome to the boards!!&nbsp; We are really lacking on this topic in here - so its fresh to see you....<br /><br />I know there are a few people in here that could learn some ish from you...</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>daps</b> on <b>August 09, 2007, 10:56:34 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">i know i can google this stuff, but in laymen&#039;s terms, can you break down definitions for mixing and&nbsp; mastering? and how the hell did dilla get the drums on &quot;the light&quot; to be so loud?</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>DAWHUD</b> on <b>August 10, 2007, 06:39:54 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Mastering is the little tinkering you do after your final mix is finished.&nbsp; They fix all the spaces inbetween the songs, EQ all the tracks and compress/boost the levels of all the songs to make them sound uniform. <br /><br />Mixing is just that... mixing.&nbsp; Get all the levels and tracks to sound right together.&nbsp; You may need to compress your vocals, add reverb, distortion... all that ish.&nbsp; Here&#039;s the thing DON&#039;T PUT YOUR ISH IN THE RED!!!!&nbsp; You may think your ish doesn&#039;t sound loud enough, but by pushing you tracks into the red is just going to make it sound like garbage later on.&nbsp; Mastering at that point would most likely just make you tracks sound worse.<br /><br />Not to diss, but I noticed a bit on &quot;the long march&quot; where Barry didn&#039;t cut out the little clip at the begining of one of the songs.&nbsp; I don&#039;t remember what song it was, but you can hear the mic a split second before the beat starts.<br /><br />I myself have been juggling many hats as I&#039;ve been working on my LP.&nbsp; (writing, producing, djing, recording and mixing)&nbsp; But...&nbsp; I&#039;m going to pass off my mastering to someone else; because I&#039;d rather have someone with the knoledge bust it out.&nbsp; Plus HAVING A DIFFERENT SET OF EARS HELPS SOOOO MUCH.<br /><br />I&#039;m working with LROX from <b>www.redsecta.com</b>.&nbsp; I met him through the ughh producer board.&nbsp; Good guy, knows his ish, good prices and he&#039;s willing to help you out.&nbsp; I don&#039;t know how many times I&#039;ve hit him up about how to do this or that.&nbsp; He&#039;s been basically guiding me through my final mix before I send him the finished product.&nbsp; <b>AND HERE&#039;S THE BEST PART</b>, he just works on hip hop, so he knows the way it shoulod sound.&nbsp; Like I told him that I want my LP to have a rugged basement vibe and he knew exactly what I ment.&nbsp; Don&#039;t get me wrong Barry IS AMAZING, but the man mostly works on rock music and has a background in rock music.&nbsp; <b>(PLUS I&#039;M SAVING ABOUT $1,000 by going to LROX) </b> But, at the same time I can&#039;t sit in on the mastering... but also sometimes douch bags will waste your time at the session while they go on some tangent about how MP3&#039;s are soooooo inferior to other mediums of music.&nbsp; (I&#039;ve head the stories)<br /><br /></div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>daps</b> on <b>August 10, 2007, 08:38:18 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">thanks dood. i always felt that dre&#039;s mastering was always on point...like in the 90&#039;s, his shit was always louder. and i think the cut you&#039;re talking about is the title track &quot;the long march&quot;...geo comes in with the pronunciation of &quot;L&quot; cut off.<br /><br />so with m ixing, what equipment do you use to make sure the shit doesn&#039;t go into the red? the obvious answer would be mixing board, but if there&#039;s any other components, i&#039;d like to know.</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>DAWHUD</b> on <b>August 10, 2007, 01:38:26 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Well... with programs like Cubase, Protools, Acid etc. you basically have everything in one box.&nbsp; Later on you might need to get some plugins, but it&#039;s basically all there.&nbsp; Your main weapon of choice will be your compressor.&nbsp; Basically what it does is make it so all the peaks and low parts have a uniform sound.&nbsp; (so you&#039;re not uber loud at one part and then quiet the next)&nbsp; But be careful, if you compress the crap out of your vocals they&#039;ll have no &quot;umph&quot; to them.&nbsp; Also another key is a high end filter or EQ.&nbsp; You&#039;ll use this to get all the SSSSSSSSSSS&#039;s out of the vocals, so it doesn&#039;t sound like you drank a lot of booze.&nbsp; But, once again don&#039;t go crazy; because you can actually make it worse if you over do it.<br /><br />Your S&#039;s and P sounds are what are going to peak your levels the most.&nbsp; A popper stopper will help with that, but wont necessairly get rid of everything when you&#039;re recording.</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>martyfever</b> on <b>August 10, 2007, 03:52:16 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Hi Jawn, I&#039;d be happy to give lessons, my only problem is I&#039;m short on time, but feel free to call me at Jupiter Studios next week 206 6331363 and we can talk about what it is that is giving you problems.<br />&nbsp; Most of what Dawhud says is right, it&#039;s basically good advice(especially the stuff about gain structure/clipping). You don&#039;t have to have amazing gear to make a great record. Sabzi has very basic, simple gear and makes great sounding music, he&#039;s very careful about picking good sounds and arranging them well, I know he doesn&#039;t use many plugins and doesn&#039;t even own a compressor. His tracks sounded good before I got involved. It does make huge difference mixing through a great analogue console, you can get separation, low end and bite that you definately can&#039;t get when staying in the computer.<br />&nbsp; Daps, part of getting the drums so loud is just balancing and EQ, but it is partly due to paralell compression (bussing out to a separate compressor and then blending it back in with the mix). This can work very well but you have to be careful about using a compressor that doesn&#039;t introduce massive phase shift, which can cancel out the original signal in certain frequency bands. Trying to do this in software can be tricky due to the latency and phase shift of the plug-in that may be on the compressor buss, but it&#039;s worth fooling around with.<br />Martin</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Jawn</b> on <b>August 10, 2007, 04:03:30 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;"><div class="quoteheader">Quote from: martyfever on August 10, 2007, 03:52:16 pm</div><div class="quote">Hi Jawn, I&#039;d be happy to give lessons, my only problem is I&#039;m short on time, but feel free to call me at Jupiter Studios next week 206 6331363 and we can talk about what it is that is giving you problems.</div><br /><br />I just need some tips on mixing. I&#039;ve copped a couple tutorial DVDs on scratching, but that doesn&#039;t really concern me so much as entertaining a crowd. Heads keep asking me if I&#039;ve produced any beats or material, and I can&#039;t keep telling them &quot;not yet&quot; if I want the town to take me seriously some day. Last year I&#039;d spend hours locked inside my bedroom practicing (with minimal equipment, too -- just a few beat records, some old funk singles and the same basic Numark tables I use today), but lately school and work have tightened up my schedule. <br /><br />I&#039;d really appreciate a few weeks worth of simple mixing and blending lessons. That way, even if I can&#039;t scratch like Q-Bert, at least I can rock a few parties and start building a rep.</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>DAWHUD</b> on <b>August 10, 2007, 05:42:00 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">I think your talking more about djing as to mixing.&nbsp; That&#039;s a whole different beast.&nbsp; Get a BPM counter, map out all your records and practice all your cuts.&nbsp; Start with babies, chops, flares, transforms, etc.</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>erik?</b> on <b>August 10, 2007, 08:42:31 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">not to burst ya bubble jawn but hes right....this dude is talking about mixing down an album to make sure the beat isnt to quiet the vocals arent too loud the sound quality is clear and not muffled..etc etc.....<br /><br />like if you listen to the CM album that came out first, then listen to the &quot;re-mastered one&quot; listen to both on somewhat decent speakers and you will deff be able to tell a difference...i could even tell a difference in my stock car stereo..<br /><br />props to marty for the quality of the mixing/mastering.</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>MTK</b> on <b>August 10, 2007, 11:58:07 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Mixing and mastering is vital.&nbsp; Not too much bass, not too much treble, the perfect balance.&nbsp; <br /><br />You would be amazed at how poorly mixed a lot of producers beats are on the beat CD&#039;s they send out.&nbsp; Muddy as all hell.<br /><br /></div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>martyfever</b> on <b>August 11, 2007, 12:11:55 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Thanks for the props Eric - you are right, I specialise in the producing/recording/mixing and mastering side of things- I certainly don&#039;t have DJing skills.<br />MTK you are right too,&nbsp; you are probably better off sourcing and creating your own beats and phrases, a lot of those production houses churn out shitty generic crap just to get $ out of you.<br />Martin</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>shawn stone</b> on <b>August 11, 2007, 01:41:54 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">here ya go jawn, perfect example of a track that needs to be mixxed.<br /><br />http://www.zshare.net/audio/3083824782fae0/</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>coo-blah-kai</b> on <b>August 11, 2007, 01:55:42 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">it&#039;s what makes or breaks any album, especially in hip hop.... </div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>martyfever</b> on <b>August 11, 2007, 02:16:42 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Quality of the music comes first but I agree after that the sound quality is vital. You have to remember that when you put your music out there it is being judged next to everything else, and a lot of those CD&#039;s (especially majors) have had huge amounts of time and money ($200K+) spent on them. Now I don&#039;t believe that you need to spend an obscene amount of $ on a record ( I&#039;ve consistently proved that you don&#039;t need to.) But there are good reasons why some records sound better/louder/more dynamic than others. You&#039;d be surprised how much difference can be&nbsp; made with a speedy mix and state of the art mastering, if the artist has their shit together it&#039;s much quicker and cheaper than you might think..<br />Martin</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>j.xavier</b> on <b>August 13, 2007, 08:49:29 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">What does someone like you charge to mix and master someone elses tracks?</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>shawn stone</b> on <b>August 13, 2007, 08:50:38 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">xavier&#039;s question reinstated.. and also.. do you do recording?</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>martyfever</b> on <b>August 13, 2007, 09:19:14 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">I do record, mix and Master. I don&#039;t normally charge by the song but by the hour or day. Label rates are quite high but I charge a lower rate for indie/self financed projects - $65 per hour or $600 for a 10 hour day. I know this is not cheap but for this you are getting a world class studio with $500K invested in it and 20 years of experience. Mixing a Hip-Hop record is much quicker than mxing a rock record, 1-2 hours per song, so a whole record can be mixed to a very high standard of kick ass in 2 days. <br />&nbsp;  I can Master most full length Lp&#039;s for under $500 including materials. If you are nervous about spending the $ then we could always do 1 song as a test so you can hear the difference it makes. (this is what I did for Sabzi then after that he was hooked!). what makes the mixing go smoothly (and ultimately saves you $),&nbsp; is to have your tracks/sessions organised well without tons of out-takes and junk in them that has to be sorted/edited etc. I can advise on all the technical/cross platform stuff that needs to take place before a session if anyone needs it.<br />hope this helps,<br />Martin</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>j.xavier</b> on <b>August 14, 2007, 12:44:26 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">For someone looking to dabble a bit in this area, or at least get down the basics--where do you suggest one starts?</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>hypknowledge*</b> on <b>August 14, 2007, 03:51:29 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">ear training&nbsp; ;D</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>martyfever</b> on <b>August 15, 2007, 12:37:32 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">If you don&#039;t have much experience the best way is to just pick up a simple recording program and a cheap audio interface and just record, record&nbsp; and record. That&#039;s the best way to train your ears. Record yourself and anyone who will let you record them. You could go to school for it (I didn&#039;t, I have no formal training at all ), some courses are better than others, but the best way is to pick it up yourself and experiment. Don&#039;t get caught up in trying to make just a few tracks perfect- it will never happen, Don&#039;t beat yourself up if it doesn&#039;t turn out the way you want it to be, just start something new instead. Write as many things as you can, you&#039;ll learn more that way and you&#039;ll be more creative. <br />Martin</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>coo-blah-kai</b> on <b>August 15, 2007, 11:25:18 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;"><div class="quoteheader">Quote from: martyfever on August 13, 2007, 09:19:14 pm</div><div class="quote">I do record, mix and Master. I don&#039;t normally charge by the song but by the hour or day. Label rates are quite high but I charge a lower rate for indie/self financed projects - $65 per hour or $600 for a 10 hour day. <br /></div><br />*Opens wallet and dust puffs from the seams.* slowly walks away...... </div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>martyfever</b> on <b>August 19, 2007, 04:54:39 pm</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Yep, the downside of having such a facilty is that you have to keep your rates at a certain level or you can&#039;t keep your doors open. I know it&#039;s out of reach for some but for those that can afford it they find that it&#039;s a worthwhile investment in their record. Alternately there are other studios/mixers out there who are cheaper but can still help elevate the quality of you project, you may have to look around before you find someone whose judgement you trust.<br />&nbsp; Props to Sabzi, Geo and the full band for yesterdays&#039; performance at the KEXP BBQ- I really enjoyed mixing that one.<br />Martin</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>Infi-Note</b> on <b>August 29, 2007, 09:42:17 am</b>
					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">I paid 40,000 dollars to learn how to mix and master my own beats, but even though I got a degree, I still think only a few people can really master music.&nbsp; I think it takes a really well trained and taken care of set of ears to master a song....not to mention some headphones and moniters you can trust!&nbsp; I feel like my mixes are on point, but i would still pass them off to someone else to master them.</div>
					<br />
					<hr size="2" width="100%" />
					Title: <b>Re: Mixing and Mastering</b><br />
					Post by: <b>martyfever</b> on <b>August 31, 2007, 01:10:48 am</b>
